Healthcare De-prioritization / Triage Bias Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V65.49 (Unsatisfactory relationship with medical care)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Under-treated pain, Dismissal frustration, Triage delay

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-021
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of unmanaged physical pain and severe emotional distress following repeated dismissal at a local emergency department, requiring immediate clinical stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, unremitting abdominal and pelvic pain that was previously dismissed as anxiety by another service, accompanied by acute panic and despair. Immediate face-to-face assessment was critical to provide adequate analgesia and rule out an acute surgical emergency that may have been missed due to triage bias, fulfilling criteria for urgent out-of-hours intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am in agony, but because I was crying, the doctor told me it was just a panic attack and sent me home without even examining my stomach.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a three-month subjective experience of having severe pain consistently under-treated by clinicians. This evening, the patient presented to an emergency department with acute, worsening pelvic pain but experienced a profound triage delay and subsequent dismissal. The patient describes repeated instances of complex physical symptoms being inappropriately attributed to anxiety, leading to a pervasive feeling of being deemed 'less worthy' of urgent medical resources. Over the last 12 hours, this resulted in profound helplessness, somatization of untreated physical issues due to systemic dismissal, and escalating emotional distress manifesting as tearfulness and anger. The spouse notes the patient has been frequent cycling through different providers seeking a basic diagnostic workup, resulting in chronic self-doubt regarding the validity of their own physical suffering and a clear deterioration of the primary medical condition due to delayed intervention.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; writhing in bed, unable to stand upright due to pain and distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of preventable morbidity due to documented systemic medical neglect and subsequent patient reluctance to seek further emergency care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/90 | HR: 112 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, diaphoretic, guarding their abdomen, and acutely tearful.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory: Tachypneic secondary to pain; clear air entry bilaterally. Abdomen/Pelvis: Significant tenderness to deep palpation in the bilateral lower quadrants with mild voluntary guarding. No rebound tenderness or rigidity. Bowel sounds are present. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. The patient exhibits profound dismissal frustration during the examination, repeatedly asking if the clinician actually believes their pain is real. The clinical picture is dominated by clear evidence of under-treated pain and systemic triage delay.
Environmental Observations: Discharge paperwork from the local emergency department is crumpled on the floor, listing only a diagnosis of "anxiety/hyperventilation."
Medication Reconciliation: No analgesia prescribed or taken in the last 8 hours; patient states they were told to "just go home and relax."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Healthcare De-prioritization / Triage Bias Distress - Adjustment Disorder - V65.49 (Unsatisfactory relationship with medical care) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ruptured Ovarian Cyst / Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of acute peritoneal signs (rigidity, rebound), normal temperature, and the gradual (rather than sudden, explosive) worsening of the pain over the preceding 24 hours.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely as the pain is bilateral rather than migrating to the right lower quadrant, and there is no associated nausea, vomiting, or significant fever.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, because the anxiety and tachycardia are secondary physiologic responses to the acutely unmanaged organic pelvic pain and the psychological trauma of medical dismissal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, unmanaged pelvic pain, reactive tachycardia (HR 112), and elevated blood pressure (148/90) following an inappropriate discharge from an emergency facility due to documented triage bias. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely essential to perform a thorough, unbiased physical examination, definitively rule out an acute surgical abdomen, and administer the urgently required analgesia that had been wrongly withheld. Delaying this assessment posed a high risk of both severe, prolonged physical suffering and potential progression of an undiagnosed underlying condition, firmly warranting urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and oral paracetamol 1000mg to immediately address the severe acute pain. Advised the usual GP to conduct a chart review demonstrating a disproportionate delay in time-to-analgesia compared to standard triage protocols, and to arrange an urgent outpatient pelvic ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the physical pain while awaiting scans, utilize a specific hot-compress protocol: apply the heat pack for exactly 20 minutes, then remove it for 20 minutes; charting this cycle on paper provides objective proof of active pain management and restores a sense of bodily control. To overcome dismissal frustration during future visits, utilize a symptom diary using a strict 1-10 pain scale without emotional descriptors; presenting pure, objective data limits the ability of biased providers to attribute symptoms to anxiety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized patient advocacy group to assist with navigating future healthcare encounters.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to review the efficacy of the analgesia and expedite the imaging referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to a different Emergency Department (accompanied by a patient advocate) or contact 000 if experiencing sudden, unbearable stabbing pain, a fever above 38.5°C, or inability to pass urine.

